<h2 class="font-headline-md text-headline-md text-primary mb-4">Common Causes of Swelling Near the Jawline</h2>
</div>
<div class="grid md:grid-cols-3 gap-8">
<div class="bg-surface-container-lowest p-8 rounded-xl shadow-sm border border-surface-variant"><span class="material-symbols-outlined text-4xl text-secondary mb-4 icon-fill-1">healing</span><h3 class="font-headline-sm text-xl text-primary mb-3">Submandibular Space Infection</h3><p class="text-on-surface-variant">A dental infection from a lower tooth spreading below the mylohyoid muscle — causes swelling under the chin and along the jawline. Can progress to Ludwig's angina (bilateral floor-of-mouth swelling) which is a life-threatening airway emergency. <a href="severe-tooth-infection-treatment-hyderabad.php" class="text-secondary underline hover:text-primary">Severe tooth infection</a> covers this in detail.</p></div>
<div class="bg-surface-container-lowest p-8 rounded-xl shadow-sm border border-surface-variant"><span class="material-symbols-outlined text-4xl text-secondary mb-4 icon-fill-1">healing</span><h3 class="font-headline-sm text-xl text-primary mb-3">Submandibular Sialadenitis</h3><p class="text-on-surface-variant">Infection or inflammation of the submandibular salivary gland — causes a tender swelling below the jaw angle. Often associated with a salivary stone (sialolith) blocking the duct. <a href="mouth-infection-treatment-hyderabad.php" class="text-secondary underline hover:text-primary">Mouth infection</a> covers salivary gland infections.</p></div>
<div class="bg-surface-container-lowest p-8 rounded-xl shadow-sm border border-surface-variant"><span class="material-symbols-outlined text-4xl text-secondary mb-4 icon-fill-1">healing</span><h3 class="font-headline-sm text-xl text-primary mb-3">Submandibular Lymphadenitis</h3><p class="text-on-surface-variant">Enlarged lymph nodes under the jaw from reactive inflammation (infection response) or, rarely, lymphoma or metastatic cancer. Reactive lymph nodes are common from dental infections, tonsillitis, or skin infections of the face and scalp.</p></div>
<div class="bg-surface-container-lowest p-8 rounded-xl shadow-sm border border-surface-variant"><span class="material-symbols-outlined text-4xl text-secondary mb-4 icon-fill-1">healing</span><h3 class="font-headline-sm text-xl text-primary mb-3">Cervical Lymph Node Abscess</h3><p class="text-on-surface-variant">An abscess within a cervical lymph node — causes a painful, firm, red swelling in the neck near the jawline. Requires surgical drainage and antibiotics. Tuberculous lymphadenitis is a specific cause in endemic areas.</p></div>
<div class="bg-surface-container-lowest p-8 rounded-xl shadow-sm border border-surface-variant"><span class="material-symbols-outlined text-4xl text-secondary mb-4 icon-fill-1">healing</span><h3 class="font-headline-sm text-xl text-primary mb-3">Mandibular Osteomyelitis</h3><p class="text-on-surface-variant">Infection of the jawbone — causes jaw pain, swelling, and potentially facial asymmetry. Often originates from a dental infection spreading into the bone. Requires prolonged IV antibiotics and possible surgical debridement. <a href="infected-jaw-treatment-hyderabad.php" class="text-secondary underline hover:text-primary">Infected jaw</a> page has full details.</p></div>
<div class="bg-surface-container-lowest p-8 rounded-xl shadow-sm border border-surface-variant"><span class="material-symbols-outlined text-4xl text-secondary mb-4 icon-fill-1">healing</span><h3 class="font-headline-sm text-xl text-primary mb-3">TMJ Inflammation / Arthritis</h3><p class="text-on-surface-variant">The temporomandibular joint (TMJ) at the jawline can become inflamed from arthritis, infection, or referred pain from teeth. Causes jawline pain, joint clicking, and limited mouth opening. May be confused with infection — our examination differentiates these conditions.</p></div>
</div>
