<div class="space-y-4">
<details class="group bg-surface-container-lowest border border-surface-variant rounded-lg [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
<summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-6 cursor-pointer font-semibold text-primary">Why is my jawline swollen near a molar tooth?<span class="material-symbols-outlined transition duration-300 group-open:-rotate-180">expand_more</span></summary>
<div class="px-6 pb-6 text-on-surface-variant text-sm">A lower molar tooth infection commonly causes jawline swelling because the tooth roots are very close to the outer jawbone surface. Infection from the tooth can spread directly into the surrounding soft tissue (submandibular space), causing the characteristic "submandibular swelling" under the jawline. The tooth is almost always the source — treating the swelling without treating the tooth leads to recurrence.</div>
</details>
<details class="group bg-surface-container-lowest border border-surface-variant rounded-lg [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
<summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-6 cursor-pointer font-semibold text-primary">Could a swollen jawline lymph node be cancer?<span class="material-symbols-outlined transition duration-300 group-open:-rotate-180">expand_more</span></summary>
<div class="px-6 pb-6 text-on-surface-variant text-sm">Most jawline lymph node swellings are reactive — enlarged in response to an infection in the head and neck region (dental, tonsillar, or skin infections). However, persistent, painless, hard, or progressively enlarging lymph nodes in the jawline/neck require biopsy to rule out lymphoma or metastatic cancer. Our surgeons refer for biopsy when clinical features suggest this possibility.</div>
</details>
<details class="group bg-surface-container-lowest border border-surface-variant rounded-lg [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
<summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-6 cursor-pointer font-semibold text-primary">Can jawline swelling affect my ability to breathe?<span class="material-symbols-outlined transition duration-300 group-open:-rotate-180">expand_more</span></summary>
<div class="px-6 pb-6 text-on-surface-variant text-sm">Yes — severe infections spreading from lower teeth into the submandibular and floor-of-mouth spaces can cause Ludwig's angina, a life-threatening bilateral swelling of the floor of the mouth that pushes the tongue upward and can obstruct the airway. This is why spreading jawline swelling should be treated as a medical emergency. CARE Hospitals has ICU and emergency airway management facilities.</div>
</details>
</div>
